SU(2) compound-square sign-gauge audit

COMMAND
make -f Makefile.research probe_su2_compound_gauge
./probe_su2_compound_gauge 12

OUTPUT
SU2_COMPOUND_GAUGE maximum_level=24 cases=78 balanced_cases=18 boundary_compatible_cases=18 first_unbalanced_level=6 first_unbalanced_factor=2 first_boundary_conflict_level=-1 first_boundary_conflict_factor=-1 first_conflict_edge=(1,3) first_unbalanced_cycle=(0,2)-[1]-(0,1)-[-1]-(1,2)-[1]-(0,2);sign_product=-1 result=FAIL_NONNEGATIVE_BOUNDARY_GAUGE

METHOD
For every half-level 1<=K<=12 and every nontrivial half-label
1<=Q<=K, the audit constructs the exact fusion matrix N_Q and the
second compound

  G_Q=C_2(N_Q^2)=C_2(N_Q)^2.

It solves the signed-graph consistency equations for a diagonal
gauge with signs in {+1,-1}.  A consistent gauge would make every
nonzero entry of G_Q nonnegative.  When consistency fails, the audit
reconstructs an exact signed cycle.

The first failure is at K=3,Q=1.  In the wedge basis it is the triangle

  (0,2) --[+1]-- (0,1) --[-1]-- (1,2) --[+1]-- (0,2).

Its edge-sign product is -1, which proves that no diagonal sign gauge
can make this G_Q entrywise nonnegative.  The bounded sweep is a route
audit; the displayed triangle is the exact obstruction.

COMPILER FLAGS
g++ -O2 -std=c++20 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion \
  -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror \
  probe_su2_compound_gauge.cpp \
  -o probe_su2_compound_gauge

